# A return type may be optional

**DONE.** Closed before these files carried a status word. The `.done`
suffix is the record; this line is that same answer in the second place
the check reads, and no new judgement.

Found binding `queryPathFromHashPart`. It answers with
`std::optional<StorePath>`, and libstore does that all over: "the
store does not have it" is a normal answer, not a failure.

The surface could not say it. A method returning `StorePath | None`
got no rpc, and the manifest blamed the wrong thing:

    StorePath | None is not in the manifest, so it has no wire policy
    (an excluded base class, most likely - see tasks/018)

The type was fine. Only optionality was missing.

## It needs no new wire machinery

A protobuf message field HAS presence. An unset one IS the None.

That is the same bit `_wire_fields` already reads with a trailing `?`,
and the same bit `PathInfo.deriver` rides on. What is new is only
where it is SAID: in the annotation, where a typechecker reads it too,
rather than in a tuple of strings the binding writes by hand.

Two spellings for one idea, then, and deliberately. `StorePath?` is
not an annotation, and a return type has to be one.

So the schema builds the field for `T` and stops; the codec splits the
annotation at both ends. `decode` ORs the declared optionality with
the flag a caller may already pass, so a caller who knows need not
read the type and a type that says so need not be told twice.

## Three things it may not be, for three reasons

**A scalar or an enum.** proto3 gives them no presence, so an absent
one and a default one are the same bytes. This is the same wall the
`?` marker hits on a scalar FIELD, and it is still there.

**A container.** A repeated field has no presence and needs none: an
absent one IS an empty one. Declaring `list[T] | None` promises a
distinction that cannot exist, and the right answer is to return the
container empty. 041 settled the parameter half of this the same way.

**A union of two real types.** Presence is one bit. It separates one
type from nothing, and it has no way to say which of two arms a field
holds.

## The fourth refusal is not about the wire

A WRAPPED `T` - a proxy - is refused for every surface at once, in
`check_optional_contract` rather than in `wire_blocker`.

The wire could almost carry it: a Handle is a message and has
presence. What has no answer is the layer above. Every layer adopts a
returned proxy into a runner - the async wrapper writes `AsyncX(result,
self._runner)`, the server puts a handle, the client builds a proxy -
and none of them adopts nothing.

Refusing it only on the wire would have left the in-process wrapper
handing back a bare sync object with no runner attached: accepted,
built, and wrong at the first await. That is the same
accepted-then-explodes shape a proxy in `_wire_fields` had, and it is
refused in the same place, for the same reason.

## Done

`Store.query_path_from_hash_part(hash_part) -> StorePath | None`. The
shim carries the optional across as a null pointer, because Cython
cannot hold a `std::optional` of a type with no default constructor -
the same reason `parse_store_path` returns one.

Both arms are asserted over the wire, and only one of them is
interesting: a missing None looks exactly like a default-built object,
which for a StorePath is an empty base name that raises.

## Still open

The optional SCALAR. A `str | None` return and a `("x", "str?")` field
hit the same wall from two directions, and both are refused rather
than wrong. Closing it means synthesising `proto3_optional` fields and
teaching the codec `HasField` for scalars - which changes the wire,
and which nothing yet needs.
